# Code organization

- Apply this rule whenever writing, changing or reviewing code.
- Before adding code, inspect the relevant folder structure and naming conventions;
  reuse existing components, packages and abstractions where they fit.
- Place each feature in its appropriate module/package and folder. Group code by
  feature and responsibility, following the project's established architecture.
- Give each component, class or module one clear responsibility. Split unrelated
  responsibilities into focused files instead of growing a single screen or class.
- Separate UI rendering, state/orchestration, business rules and data access where
  those responsibilities exist. Keep business rules independent of UI and storage.
- For example, a meal screen renders UI, a state holder coordinates interactions,
  a planning service applies menu rules, and a repository handles persistence.
- Keep feature-specific helpers inside their feature; move code into shared
  packages only when it has a clear shared purpose. Avoid generic dumping grounds.
- Use explicit interfaces at module boundaries and avoid circular dependencies.
- Keep the structure proportional to the task: do not create empty layers, needless
  classes or premature abstractions merely to increase the number of files.
- Before finishing, check file placement, responsibility boundaries, duplicate
  logic and affected imports; run relevant build/tests when applicable.
